response:
 The polynomial $x^6 + ax + b$ has to be divisible by $x^2 - 2x - 1.$ Let's find the roots of the divisor i.e., $x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0.$
\begin{align*}
x &= \frac{2 \pm \sqrt{(-2)^2 - 4 \cdot 1 \cdot (-1)}}{2 \cdot 1}\\
&= \frac{2 \pm 2\sqrt{2}}{2}\\
&= 1 \pm \sqrt{2}\\
\end{align*}
The remainder equals 0 when $x = 1 + \sqrt{2}$ or $x = 1 - \sqrt{2}.$
\begin{align*}
\therefore (1 + \sqrt{2})^6 + a(1 + \sqrt{2}) + b = 0\\
(1 - \sqrt{2})^6 + a(1 - \sqrt{2}) + b = 0\\
\therefore a + b &= 6\\
\end{align*}
</think> <answer> $6$ </answer>

